# SESSION_PROTOCOL

Why every session in {{ repo }} ends the way `AGENTS.md` — and, for a session running
as an installed skill, the skill's own instructions — say it should. Those carry the
operational short form, read every reply; this file is where the reasoning behind it
lives once, so neither has to restate it.

## Why concise, by default

Default AI verbosity costs the reader attention they didn't agree to spend. That
isn't a nice-to-have setting — it ships on, and this is the one place that says why
instead of just asserting it.

**Always:** concise, complete, straight to the point. State results and decisions
directly. No restating the request, no narrating steps about to be taken, no padding.

## The status block

Every substantive reply ends with a compact block. Omit a line entirely when there's
nothing to report for it — never print "None" as filler:

- **Questions pending** — brief bullets, awaiting {{ assignee }}.
- **Progress** — % complete on the current task or milestone.
- **Git/PR status** — branch, clean or uncommitted, PR number and state.
- **Expected next input** — what {{ assignee }} should say next. If the session can
  compact its own context or start fresh, and the conversation has grown long, say so
  here — capability-gated phrasing, never a named command.

## Ending a session

At a real stopping point — not after every message:

- **Done this session** — brief, complete bullets.
- **Planned for next session** — brief, complete bullets.
- **Memory handoff** — enough state that the next session can pick up cold, written
  to **`{{ memoryFile }}`** and nowhere else.

Push everything, update the issue and PR, leave the working tree clean, before
signing off. This is a handoff, not a pause.

## Why memory is a file, never a GitHub comment

Two different things get updated at the end of a session, and they don't belong in
the same place:

- **Issue and PR *fields*** — status, labels, milestone, assignee, links — are real
  project data. They keep being updated on GitHub, exactly as `WORKFLOW.md` describes.
- **An agent's running account of its own sessions** — what it tried, what it
  learned, where it stopped — is not project data. It goes in `{{ memoryFile }}`,
  never to an issue comment, a PR comment, or a board note.

GitHub stays exactly as readable to a human as it would be if no agent had ever
touched this repo. This is not a preference a capable agent gets to override — there
is no GitHub-comment option, for any target, regardless of what it's able to do.

## What isn't checkable from inside a session

Nothing in this repo mechanically verifies that a reply actually ended with the
status block above — there's no test that can observe a live conversation. The
closest thing to a gate is the discipline of following this file itself, every reply,
including the one that just generated it.
